//? Title: Lucas primality by exact multiplicative order
//? Subtitle: Actual factorization, modular-power certificates and explicit inconclusive budgets
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A Fermat congruence alone does not certify primality. With the complete prime
factorization of n-minus1, the Lucas order criterion adds proper-order checks
that force an element to have order n-minus1 and hence prove n prime. We retain
the original1009 witness and least-witness plot, move all factoring/search logic
into the native library, and report bounded exhaustion separately from
compositeness.
)

\toc

> A hand-checkable order certificate for seven

Suppose n>2 and the complete distinct prime divisors of n−1 are known. If
a^(n−1)=1 modulo n, then a is a unit and its multiplicative order d divides n−1.
If, for every prime q dividing n−1, a^((n−1)/q) differs from1 modulo n, no prime
factor can be removed from n−1 while remaining a multiple of d. Thus d=n−1.
Since d divides the unit-group size phi(n), and phi(n)<=n−1 with equality only
for prime n, the conditions prove primality. A failed proper-order check rejects
that base, not the number. A failed Fermat congruence does prove compositeness.

For n=7, n−1=2*3. Base2 satisfies the Fermat congruence but its cube is1
modulo7, so it has insufficient order. Base3 has sixth power1, cube6 and square2
modulo7; both proper-order residues differ from1, giving an exact order-six
certificate. The table/diagram show the native producer’s actual checks, with no
report-local exponentiation or witness search.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lucas_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::lucas_primality_trace(7);
    std::print("| actual base | factor0 means Fermat | actual exponent | actual residue | actual condition " "passed |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (auto &p : t.powers)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", p.base, p.factor, p.exponent,
                   p.residue, p.passed);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("factor", {0, 0}, "Actual complete factors\n6 = 2 times3").node(
        "reject", {6, 0},
        std::format("Actual rejected base{}\nfailed order factor{}",
                    t.bases.front().base, t.bases.front().rejected_factor)).node("proof", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual full-order witness{}\nconclusive prime {}", t.analysis.witness, t.analysis.prime)).edge("factor", "reject").edge("reject", "proof");
    d.save_svg("build/lucas_tiny.svg");
}
```
