> Explicitly inconclusive work limits

Trial division has worst-case O(sqrt(n)) candidate tests, and each examined base
requests at most1+omega(n−1) modular powers, each with O(log(n)) exact modular
products. A proof criterion should not be confused with an efficient discovery
method. Two independent budgets limit actual divisor tests and actual examined
bases; their exhaustion returns FactorBudget or BaseBudget with
conclusive=false. A false prime flag on such an outcome is not a composite
certificate. Zero work budgets mean no permitted work in that phase, not
unlimited execution.

The ordinary bool method shares the producer with one million divisor tests
and100000 bases, and throws on inconclusive exhaustion. This bounds previously
unbounded work while preserving its conclusive mathematical booleans. Actual
Fermat failures now stop immediately instead of scanning pointless later bases.
The complete trace owns one candidate word plus each actual factor-removal,
modular-power and base-outcome record; its optional million-record capture cap
counts logical records before append, excluding fields, factor/result copies,
capacities, runtime copies and bytes. Zero disables only that optional capture
cap. Work-limit exhaustion returns the complete bounded history; capture-limit
exhaustion throws rather than returning a misleading prefix.

The independent controls below use7 to separate partial factorization, completed
factorization with no permitted base, one rejected base, and a complete
certificate. They do not replay prefixes to build any trace. Inputs0/1 and even
composites are conclusive by trivial filters;2 is conclusive prime without
either work phase. Full unsigned arithmetic is checked by a separate2^61−1
native control, but no promise is made that every64-bit candidate can finish
trial division within the default budget. No external dataset is relevant to
this exact arithmetic study.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lucas_budgets.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| independent divisor budget | independent base budget | actual stop enum | actual " "factorization complete | actual conclusive | actual prime | actual examined bases |\n| ---: " "| ---: | ---: | --- | --- | --- | ---: |\n");
    std::size_t i = 0;
    for (auto[divisors, bases] : std::vector<std::pair<std::size_t, std::size_t>>{{0, 100}, {100, 0}, {100, 1}, {100, 2}}) {
        auto a = dm::algorithms::lucas_primality_analyze(7, divisors, bases);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", divisors, bases,
                   int(a.stop), a.factorization_complete, a.conclusive, a.prime,
                   a.work.bases);
        x.push_back(i++);
        y.push_back(a.conclusive);
    }
    auto large =
        dm::algorithms::lucas_primality_analyze((std::uint64_t{1} << 61) - 1);
    std::print("\nActual2-to61-minus1 conclusive {}; prime {}; witness {}; actual trial tests {}; actual " "powers {}.\n",
               large.conclusive, large.prime, large.witness,
               large.work.trial_divisors, large.work.powers);
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual conclusive", {37, 99, 235}, 6).y_limits(-.1, 1.5).title("Actual work exhaustion is separate from compositeness").x_label("listed independent budget control").y_label("actual conclusive outcome: one=yes").save_svg("build/lucas_budgets.svg");
}
```
