> Verification and references

Native tests compare every candidate0..3000 with an independent trial-prime
reference, verify complete factor products and actual exact-removal states,
inspect every real power/base record and strict least-witness choice,
retain7/1009/1001 and the full-width2^61−1 control, and cover independent work
budgets, capture admission, trivial filters and census boundaries. The
ten-report batch passes 95 CTest cases, 30 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan
cases, 121 compiled and executed public examples, 601/601 scoped documentation
definitions and 11 representative hovers. Independent integer, Fraction, graph,
string and NumPy references verify 82,729 records, including the complete
original seeded LSH study. Frozen comparisons preserve ordinary legacy results;
malformed-input, sentinel-collision, ownership and explicit-budget corrections
are documented above. Thirteen checkout headers and thirteen installed headers
compile independently, and an installed-header native consumer links and runs.
Each complete report program runs against the checkout library; all four native
figures are reviewed and HTML/Markdown exports preserve computed results and
image bytes. Batch receipts: `build/mepml-conversion/batch-214-223/`.

V. R. Pratt(1975),
[Every Prime Has a Succinct Certificate|https://doi.org/10.1137/0204018], SIAM
Journal on Computing4(3),214–220, presents primality proofs based on the
complete predecessor factorization and multiplicative-order conditions. This
implementation exposes the exact Lucas order evidence but does not build Pratt’s
recursively succinct factor-primality proof tree; it discovers the factors by
checked bounded trial division.
